<?xml version="1.0" encoding="utf-8"?>
<!--
    Wi-Fi capability resources for sm8635 (arcfox / razr 50 ultra).

    Every value below is set from something the radio or the driver said, not
    from the marketing spec sheet. The evidence, captured on the running device
    with `iw phy` and from the driver ini, is quoted per entry.

    Why this file exists at all: AOSP defaults config_wifi5ghzSupport and
    config_wifi6ghzSupport to FALSE, so a device that ships no Wi-Fi RRO claims
    to be a 2.4 GHz-only radio. Nothing about STA mode notices — this device was
    happily associated at 5260 MHz with these still false, because association
    goes through the driver's own regulatory data. SoftAP is where it bites:
    ApConfigUtil.isSoftApBandSupported() consults ONLY these resources, so
    `cmd wifi start-softap ... -b 5` failed with

        E ApConfigUtil: Can not start softAp with band 5Ghz not supported.

    while the identical command with -b 2 brought up a working AP. Do not read
    that error as a driver, hostapd or regulatory problem — it is decided in the
    framework before hostapd is ever asked to do anything, and it is NOT the
    missing country code (forcing one with `cmd wifi force-country-code enabled`
    does not change it).
-->
<resources>
    <!--
        `iw phy` reports three bands:
            Band 1  2412-2484 MHz
            Band 2  5180-5900 MHz   <- STA is associated here at 5260 MHz
            Band 4  5955-7115 MHz   <- 6 GHz, up to channel 233
        and the driver ini has oem_6g_support_disable=0.
    -->
    <bool name="config_wifi5ghzSupport">true</bool>
    <bool name="config_wifi6ghzSupport">true</bool>

    <!--
        config_wifiSoftap24ghzSupported and config_wifiSoftap5ghzSupported are
        already true in AOSP defaults, so only 6 GHz needs turning on here.
    -->
    <bool name="config_wifiSoftap6ghzSupported">true</bool>

    <!--
        All three bands advertise BOTH "HE Iftypes: AP" and "EHT Iftypes: AP" in
        `iw phy`, so the radio can beacon 802.11ax and 802.11be. Confirmed from
        the other side too: hostapd's own driver capability read logs
        he_supported=1, eht_supported=1.

        Without these the AP negotiates down to 11n even when the framework's
        SoftApConfiguration asks for ax — observed directly, hostapd logging
        `getGeneration ... he_supported=1, eht_supported=1, ieee80211ax = 0,
        ieee80211be=0` and settling on wifiStandard=4 (11n) on 2.4 GHz.

        Expect 11be on a SINGLE AP only. config_wifiSoftapIeee80211beSupported is
        merely the FIRST gate in SoftApManager.is11beAllowedForThisConfiguration();
        the log line "11BE is not allowed, removing from configuration" can still
        fire afterwards, and in bridged mode always will, because
        config_wifiSoftApMaxNumberMLDSupported defaults to 0 and
        config_wifiSoftApSingleLinkMloInBridgedModeSupported to false. Measured
        single-AP 6 GHz reached wifiStandard=8, so the gate is doing real work
        here and is not dead weight.
    -->
    <bool name="config_wifiSoftapIeee80211axSupported">true</bool>
    <bool name="config_wifiSoftapIeee80211beSupported">true</bool>

    <!--
        11ac, for the same reason as 11ax above: hostapd reports vht_enabled=1 on
        5 GHz. AOSP defaults this to false too.
    -->
    <bool name="config_wifi_softap_ieee80211ac_supported">true</bool>

    <!--
        SAE is a property of the hostapd BINARY, not of the radio, and this build
        compiles it in (hostapd_cflags_default carries -DCONFIG_SAE). It has to be
        declared here or a 6 GHz AP is impossible: the WFA rules coded into
        ApConfigUtil.isSecurityTypeRestrictedFor6gBand forbid OPEN and
        WPA2-Personal on 6 GHz, so SAE is the only usable option there, and asking
        for it without this failed with "Error, SAE requires HAL support".
    -->
    <bool name="config_wifi_softap_sae_supported">true</bool>

    <!--
        ACS IS DELIBERATELY LEFT FALSE. This is a measured trade-off, not an
        oversight, and it is the opposite of what the hostapd build flags suggest
        (-DCONFIG_ACS is compiled in and the offload works on 2.4 and 5 GHz).

        Declaring ACS makes the framework pass channel 0 and let hostapd offload
        the channel survey to the driver. On 6 GHz this driver rejects the QCA ACS
        vendor attributes outright:

            hostapd: ACS: Offloading to driver
            hostapd: wlan2: nl80211: kernel reports: Unknown attribute type
            hostapd: Configured channel (0) or frequency (0) not found from the
                     channel list of the current mode (2) IEEE 802.11a
            hostapd: Could not select hw_mode and channel. (-3)

        hostapd then falls back to the 5 GHz hwmode with no channel at all and the
        AP never starts. Leaving ACS undeclared takes the other branch of
        ApConfigUtil.updateApChannelConfig, where the FRAMEWORK picks a concrete
        channel via chooseApChannel() before hostapd is called, and the driver is
        never asked to run ACS.

        Measured, same build, only this value changed, STA off and a country code
        set (all three verified as "Soft AP is started"):

            ACS declared      2.4 GHz ok (2462)   5 GHz ok (5745)   6 GHz FAILS
            ACS undeclared    2.4 GHz ok (2427)   5 GHz ok (5805)   6 GHz ok (6055)

        The 6 GHz AP comes up at wifiStandard=8, i.e. 802.11be. The benefit is the
        entire 6 GHz band. The costs, stated in full so the trade can be re-judged
        later:
          * the channel is picked by chooseApChannel() from wificond's list, at
            random, per start — no interference survey;
          * setAllowedAcsChannels is wiped for all three bands
            (ApConfigUtil.java:1043);
          * BRIDGED / dual-band SoftAP becomes impossible outright —
            ApConfigUtil.java:1546 rejects a dual-AP config whose channels are
            unspecified when ACS offload is absent. That costs nothing TODAY only
            because config_wifiBridgedSoftApSupported defaults to false and this
            overlay does not set it. Anyone turning bridged mode on must revisit
            this line first.
        If the ACS offload is ever fixed on the driver side, flip this to true and
        re-run the 6 GHz case before believing it.
    -->
    <bool name="config_wifi_softap_acs_supported">false</bool>
</resources>
